INT. LAMPLIGHTER'S WORKSHOP - DAWN

1887. London. Brass tools. Oil cans. A cold stove.

MRS. ADELAIDE PRYCE, 60s, black bombazine, stands in the doorway. Does not sit. Gloves stay on.

Across the bench: TOBIAS WREN, 60s, lamplighter. Wick scissors in hand. He trims nothing.

Silence. A kettle ticks as it cools.

ADELAIDE
You kept the shop.

TOBIAS
Rent's paid.

She touches a lamp on the shelf. Dusts her glove on it. Studies the smear.

ADELAIDE
Not by much, I'd say.

He sets the scissors down. Picks them up again.

TOBIAS
You came a long way.

ADELAIDE
The cab was warm.

A bell clangs in the street. A boy's voice, muffled through the wall.

BOY (O.S.)
Lamps, Mr. Wren! Gas is on at Fenchurch!

Tobias does not turn. Adelaide watches him not turn.

ADELAIDE
Your boy?

TOBIAS
Hired.

ADELAIDE
He shouts like his mother.

Tobias's jaw works. He crosses to the stove. Opens it. Closes it. Nothing inside.

TOBIAS
Tea?

ADELAIDE
No.

She sits. Finally. The chair creaks. He looks at the chair, then at her gloves.

TOBIAS
Take those off. You'll boil.

ADELAIDE
It's November.

TOBIAS
Still.

A long beat. She pulls one finger loose. Stops. Pushes it back.

ADELAIDE
I said what I came to say. In the letter.

TOBIAS
I burned the letter.

ADELAIDE
Then you know.

TOBIAS
I know.

The boy shouts again, further off. Fainter.

BOY (O.S.)
Lamps!

Adelaide rises. Smooths her skirt twice. Crosses to the door. Stops with her back to him.

ADELAIDE
Light one for me. On Fenchurch.

TOBIAS
It's lit.

ADELAIDE
Then light it again.

She leaves. The bell over the door swings. Tobias takes down the dusty lamp. Wipes the smear with his sleeve. Carefully.
